PRACTICAL 9: AUTO-SCALING AND MONITORING AI APP USING KUBERNETES

1. AIM
To automatically scale an AI application on Kubernetes and monitor its health and performance.

2. REQUIREMENTS
- Docker Desktop
- Minikube
- kubectl
- Previous Minikube AI app (Deployment: ai-model; Service: ai-model-service)

3. START MINIKUBE AND METRICS SERVER

Start Docker Desktop, then run:

minikube start --driver=docker
minikube addons enable metrics-server

Check the cluster:

kubectl get nodes
kubectl top nodes

4. ADD A TEST-LOAD ROUTE TO app.py

Add this route to the previous Flask app:

@app.route("/load")
def load():
    total = 0
    for i in range(1000000):
        total += i * i
    return jsonify({"status": "test completed"})

This route creates CPU work only to demonstrate autoscaling. It is not needed in a real application.

Rebuild and load the new image:

docker build -t ai-model:v2 .
minikube image load ai-model:v2

5. UPDATE THE KUBERNETES DEPLOYMENT

In deployment.yaml, use the following Deployment. Keep your existing Service named ai-model-service.

apiVersion: apps/v1
kind: Deployment
metadata:
  name: ai-model
spec:
  replicas: 1
  selector:
    matchLabels:
      app: ai-model
  template:
    metadata:
      labels:
        app: ai-model
    spec:
      containers:
      - name: ai-model
        image: ai-model:v2
        imagePullPolicy: IfNotPresent
        ports:
        - containerPort: 5000
        resources:
          requests:
            cpu: "100m"
            memory: "128Mi"
          limits:
            cpu: "500m"
            memory: "256Mi"
        readinessProbe:
          httpGet:
            path: /
            port: 5000
          periodSeconds: 5
        livenessProbe:
          httpGet:
            path: /
            port: 5000
          periodSeconds: 10

Apply the updated deployment:

kubectl apply -f deployment.yaml

6. CREATE THE AUTO-SCALER

Create a file named hpa.yaml:

apiVersion: autoscaling/v2
kind: HorizontalPodAutoscaler
metadata:
  name: ai-model-hpa
spec:
  scaleTargetRef:
    apiVersion: apps/v1
    kind: Deployment
    name: ai-model
  minReplicas: 1
  maxReplicas: 5
  metrics:
  - type: Resource
    resource:
      name: cpu
      target:
        type: Utilization
        averageUtilization: 50

Apply it:

kubectl apply -f hpa.yaml

7. GENERATE TEST LOAD

Run this command to repeatedly call the AI app:

kubectl run load-generator --image=busybox:1.36 --restart=Never -- /bin/sh -c "while true; do wget -q -O- http://ai-model-service:5000/load > /dev/null; done"

Wait about one or two minutes, then check:

kubectl get hpa
kubectl get pods -l app=ai-model

The number of AI model pods may increase when CPU usage goes above the target. Scaling depends on the available resources and amount of load.

Stop the test:

kubectl delete pod load-generator

8. MONITOR HEALTH AND PERFORMANCE

Check pods:

kubectl get pods

Check CPU and memory:

kubectl top pods
kubectl top nodes

Check autoscaler details:

kubectl describe hpa ai-model-hpa

Check application logs:

kubectl logs deployment/ai-model

Readiness probe checks whether the app can receive traffic.
Liveness probe checks whether the app is still healthy.

9. RESULT
Successfully configured Kubernetes autoscaling for the AI model and monitored pod health, CPU, memory, and scaling activity.

10. VIVA QUESTIONS

Q1. What is auto-scaling?
Ans. Automatically increasing or decreasing the number of application pods based on demand.

Q2. What is HPA?
Ans. Horizontal Pod Autoscaler automatically changes the number of pod replicas.

Q3. What is Metrics Server?
Ans. It provides CPU and memory metrics to Kubernetes.

Q4. Which command shows pod CPU and memory?
Ans. kubectl top pods

Q5. What is a readiness probe?
Ans. It checks whether a pod is ready to receive traffic.

Q6. What is a liveness probe?
Ans. It checks whether a container is healthy.

Q7. What do minReplicas and maxReplicas specify?
Ans. The minimum and maximum number of pods.

Q8. Which command displays HPA status?
Ans. kubectl get hpa

